# 3. AUTHENTICATION AND USER ROLES

Implement secure authentication using Firebase Authentication with email and password.

Implement exactly three primary roles:

## ADMINISTRATOR

Administrator capabilities:
- View system-wide document metadata.
- View user accounts.
- Manage user activation and deactivation.
- View system-wide audit events.
- View permission grant and revocation history.
- View failed authorization attempts.
- View suspicious activity.
- View audit chain verification status.
- Manage categories.
- Manage system settings.

Important:
Administrator must NOT automatically have permission to read, preview, download, edit, or delete every document.

Administrative privileges are not equivalent to document ownership.

## DOCUMENT OWNER

Owner capabilities:
- Upload documents.
- View documents they own.
- Organize documents into categories.
- Edit document metadata.
- Replace document content by creating a new version.
- Grant READ permissions.
- Grant EDIT permissions.
- Revoke previously granted permissions.
- View access history for owned documents.
- Soft-delete owned documents.
- Restore documents when retention rules permit.

Owners must not access another owner's documents without an explicit grant.

## VIEWER

Viewer capabilities:
- View documents explicitly shared with them.
- Download documents when READ permission is granted.
- View document metadata.
- View their own access history.

A viewer must not:
- Upload documents.
- Edit documents.
- Delete documents.
- Share documents with other people.
- Change permissions.
- Access documents belonging to other users without a grant.

Implement authorization using server-side role and permission checks.

Do not trust roles, document IDs, owner IDs, permission levels, or user IDs supplied by the frontend.

Use Firebase Admin SDK to verify Firebase ID tokens.

Use server-managed roles or trusted Firebase custom claims. Never allow a client to assign its own administrator role.

Provide a secure first-administrator bootstrap procedure using a one-time setup secret or trusted administrative setup script.

Do not hardcode production administrator credentials.

